+{
+ "cells": [
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "8fd00e62",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "# MILES-GUESS Classification Example Notebook\n",
+    "\n",
+    "John Schreck, David John Gagne, Charlie Becker, Gabrielle Gantos, Dhamma Kimpara, Thomas Martin\n",
+    "\n",
+    "This extends the authors' **precipitation-type** example using their bundled `data/ptype_sample.parquet`, the four original 21-level meteorological feature groups, four precipitation classes, and original YAML model settings. The deterministic model retains **four hidden layers of 6024 neurons**, balanced training, class weights, and the original **one epoch**. The evidential model retains four hidden layers of 212 neurons and the original **five epochs**. CPU training of the original deterministic architecture can require substantial memory and time.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "The original notebook loaded the deterministic sample dataset once, applied its quantile transform with `groups=[]`, and reused those data for both models. That behavior is preserved, even though the standalone evidential YAML names a different production dataset and robust scaler. Set `MILES_PTYPE_DATA` explicitly before running to evaluate another authors-provided precipitation dataset with the same schema; no generated fallback is used.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "The added evaluation uses only the reserved **test** days, rather than the validation rows used in the original evaluation cells. The original test cutoff, case-study days, training split seed, and training-only feature transformation are retained. Models are fitted with validation monitoring; test results never determine architecture, stopping, calibration, or class weights. Short demonstration runs do not reproduce the paper's fully trained results."
+   ]
