+   "source": [
+    "## Diurnal uncertainty composite following Figure 11\n",
+    "\n",
+    "This additional diagnostic follows the time-of-day presentation in **Figure 11 of [Schreck et al. (2024), *Evidential Deep Learning: Enhancing Predictive Uncertainty Estimation for Earth System Science Applications*](https://doi.org/10.1175/AIES-D-23-0093.1)**. It uses this notebook's existing evidential model and **all held-out test observations**, grouped by their source timestamps into 10-minute clock-time bins across all test dates. The original example predicts friction velocity only, so the composite has one panel. Its short training budget and original split are retained; the figure illustrates this run rather than reproducing the paper's fully trained results.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Let $B_t$ contain the held-out observations in clock-time bin $t$. The blue and orange curves show $|B_t|^{-1}\\sum_{n\\in B_t}y_n$ and $|B_t|^{-1}\\sum_{n\\in B_t}\\gamma_n$, respectively. Using the paper's Eqs. (25) and (26), the green and red curves show **conditional RMS standard deviations**:\n",
+    "\n",
+    "$$\n",
+    "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{|B_t|}\\sum_{n\\in B_t}\\mathbb{E}[\\sigma_n^2]}\n",
+    "\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n",
+    "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{|B_t|}\\sum_{n\\in B_t}\\operatorname{Var}(\\mu_n)}.\n",
+    "$$\n",
+    "\n",
+    "All quantities here have first been converted to physical target units. Averaging variances before taking their square root keeps all four curves in the same physical units. These uncertainty curves are **not confidence intervals or standard errors for the diurnal means**, and do not include between-observation variation of predicted means, so they are not the variance of the full distribution pooled within a clock-time bin. The CSV includes per-bin counts to make sampling differences visible. Missing bins remain gaps. The source timestamps have no stated timezone; none is assigned.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "The figure and its complete summary are saved as high-resolution PNG, SVG, and CSV files for further analysis. The earlier full-test error, coverage, calibration, and chronological diagnostics remain available above.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "For this original five-epoch Keras fit, a few very large predicted epistemic variances dominate the RMS curve. The full linear plot is retained. A second view uses a **symmetric-log vertical scale, linear within ±0.1 m s⁻¹**, to make all four curves visible. Both views contain exactly the same unmodified values; no observations, bins, or uncertainty values are removed or clipped. The scale change aids inspection and does not imply that the original short-run uncertainties are calibrated."
