+   "source": [
+    "# Evidential uncertainty on your own dataset\n",
+    "This guide trains a CPU evidential model, chooses its checkpoint using validation data, and evaluates the untouched test set. Its default uses the authors’ bundled Cabauw dataset, original PyTorch regression architecture and one-pass training settings. To use your own CSV, edit the next configuration cell: choose regression or classification, specify the input and target columns, and choose an appropriate split. The classification option requires your labeled CSV and starts from the authors’ PyTorch classifier configuration. The restored original notebooks remain the reference for reproducing each authors’ experiment.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "For regression, the network predicts Normal–Inverse–Gamma parameters and a Student-t predictive distribution. For classification, it predicts evidence defining a Dirichlet distribution. Aleatoric and epistemic outputs are variances. They are not interchangeable with predictive interval widths or Dempster–Shafer uncertainty mass.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "**Research use:** split independent experimental units before preprocessing. For repeated subjects, trajectories, stations or simulations, use group splitting. For forecasting, use chronological splitting. Keep the test set separate from model selection; if you tune based on its results, it becomes validation data and you need a new test set.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "The default follows the authors’ bundled data and notebook settings. Assess performance separately on your own held-out data.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "**Notation key for the paper.** We use the notation of [Schreck et al. (2024)](https://doi.org/10.1175/AIES-D-23-0093.1). For one fixed input $x_n$ and one regression target, the network outputs $(\\gamma_n,\\nu_n,\\alpha_n,\\beta_n)$, with $\\nu_n>0$, $\\alpha_n>1$, and $\\beta_n>0$. The latent mean $\\mu_n$ and latent observation-noise variance $\\sigma_n^2$ follow the NIG distribution in Eqs. (12)–(14); $\\gamma_n$ is the point prediction, not the latent random mean.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "| Quantity in the code | Paper notation | Paper equation |\n",
+    "| --- | --- | --- |\n",
+    "| NIG location, sometimes named `mu`; evaluation field `mean` | $\\mathbb{E}[\\mu_n]=\\gamma_n$ | (24) |\n",
+    "| `aleatoric` | $\\mathbb{E}[\\sigma_n^2]=\\beta_n/(\\alpha_n-1)$ | (25) |\n",
+    "| `epistemic` | $\\operatorname{Var}(\\mu_n)=\\beta_n/[\\nu_n(\\alpha_n-1)]$ | (26) |\n",
+    "| `total` | $\\mathbb{E}[\\sigma_n^2]+\\operatorname{Var}(\\mu_n)$ | (19), applied to the NIG model |\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Expectations and variances above are evaluated at the **same fixed input** under its predicted NIG distribution; they are not calculated across different test rows. Code arrays apply these formulas separately to every row and target. Predictive NLL, PIT, and intervals use the Student-t distribution in Eq. (15), with location $\\gamma_n$, $2\\alpha_n$ degrees of freedom, and squared scale $\\beta_n(1+\\nu_n)/(\\nu_n\\alpha_n)$. Its scale is not its standard deviation. Raw network parameters use the training target scale; evaluation converts locations and variances to the original target units, with variances multiplied by the squared inverse scaling factor.\n"
+   ]
+  },
