-    "#### Predict with the model"
+    "## 4. Original Gaussian ensemble across two data splits\n",
+    "\n",
+    "The Gaussian YAML specifies `n_models=1, n_splits=2`; the original loop trains those two members for one epoch each. Each member retains its own fitted input and target scalers. Members are combined **after** conversion to physical units.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Here the paper's Gaussian-model parameters are $(\\mu_{n,m},\\sigma_{n,m}^2)$ for input $x_n$ and member $m$. Unlike NIG regression, each Gaussian member predicts its mean $\\mu_{n,m}$ directly. Let $J$ be a uniformly selected member among the $M=2$ members, and write $\\bar\\mu_n=M^{-1}\\sum_m\\mu_{n,m}$. Equation (19), with the member index $J$ as the conditioning variable, gives\n",
+    "\n",
+    "$$\n",
+    "\\underbrace{\\mathbb{E}_J[\\operatorname{Var}(y_n\\mid J)]}_{\\text{aleatoric}}\n",
+    "=\\frac{1}{M}\\sum_{m=1}^{M}\\sigma_{n,m}^{2},\n",
+    "\\qquad\n",
+    "\\underbrace{\\operatorname{Var}_J(\\mathbb{E}[y_n\\mid J])}_{\\text{epistemic}}\n",
+    "=\\frac{1}{M}\\sum_{m=1}^{M}(\\mu_{n,m}-\\bar\\mu_n)^2.\n",
+    "$$\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Both terms are evaluated at the same fixed $x_n$; their sum is the total mixture variance at that input. These correspond to the `aleatoric`, `epistemic`, and `total` output fields. Population variance (`ddof=0`) is appropriate for this equally weighted finite mixture. The evaluation below computes its exact mixture NLL and numerical mixture quantiles. No test error is used to select a member.\n"
    ]
   },
