+    "This is the authors' original Cabauw example, extended with held-out evaluation. It uses the **bundled original surface-layer observations**, the same four inputs and friction-velocity target, the YAML's five 1057-unit hidden layers and dropout rates, batch size 128, Adam at 0.001, and **one pass through training data for each model**, as in the original notebook. There is no replacement dataset, shortened training run, or hyperparameter search.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Portability changes: paths work from the repository or notebook directory; CPU is the default; the configuration seed also fixes initialization and shuffling. The original code groups both splits by **year** (despite its old text saying day); that behavior is retained. Preprocessing is fitted on training data only. Test data are evaluated after both models have finished training. The deterministic L1 and evidential Lipschitz-MSE objectives are retained. The modern library converts target variances using the squared inverse scaling factor; the old `training_var` attribute is unnecessary.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Install with `python -m pip install -e \".[torch,notebooks]\"` after installing your intended CPU PyTorch build. The full original model can take several minutes on a CPU. The results demonstrate this original single-pass setup; they are not a claim of a tuned model or a reproduction of the paper's full experiment.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "\n",
+    "## Notation used in the paper\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Throughout this notebook, $n$ indexes an observation and the equations apply at its fixed input $\\mathbf{x}_n$. For multiple targets, apply them separately to each target. The NIG network outputs are $(\\gamma_n,\\nu_n,\\alpha_n,\\beta_n)$, following **Eqs. (12)–(15) and (24)–(26)** of [Schreck et al. (2024)](https://doi.org/10.1175/AIES-D-23-0093.1).\n",
+    "\n",
+    "| Paper notation | Meaning | Notebook/code correspondence |\n",
+    "| --- | --- | --- |\n",
+    "| $\\gamma_n=\\mathbb{E}[\\mu_n]$ — Eq. (24) | Predicted location / expected latent mean | Model output `gamma`; the evaluation helper also calls this parameter `mu` and returns it as `evidential_test[\"mean\"]` |\n",
+    "| $\\mu_n$ | Uncertain latent Gaussian mean | A random variable in the NIG hierarchy; distinct from the code's deterministic location variable `mu` |\n",
+    "| $\\sigma_n^2$ | Uncertain conditional observation-noise variance | A random variable in the NIG hierarchy |\n",
+    "| $\\nu_n,\\alpha_n,\\beta_n$ | Remaining NIG parameters | Arrays `nu`, `alpha`, `beta`; $\\nu_n>0$, $\\alpha_n>1$, $\\beta_n>0$ |\n",
+    "| $\\mathbb{E}[\\sigma_n^2]=\\beta_n/(\\alpha_n-1)$ — Eq. (25) | Aleatoric **variance** | `evidential_test[\"aleatoric\"]` |\n",
+    "| $\\operatorname{Var}(\\mu_n)=\\beta_n/[\\nu_n(\\alpha_n-1)]$ — Eq. (26) | Epistemic **variance** | `evidential_test[\"epistemic\"]` |\n",
+    "\n",
+    "The total predictive variance is $\\operatorname{Var}(y_n)=\\mathbb{E}[\\sigma_n^2]+\\operatorname{Var}(\\mu_n)$, by **Eq. (19)**, and is returned as `evidential_test[\"total\"]`. These expectations and variances are under the learned NIG distribution at the same input, not sample statistics across test rows. The earlier shorthand $V_{\\mathrm{aleatoric},n}$ and $V_{\\mathrm{epistemic},n}$ meant exactly $\\mathbb{E}[\\sigma_n^2]$ and $\\operatorname{Var}(\\mu_n)$; the explanations below now use the paper's notation directly.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Formulas hold in the units in which the parameters are expressed. The evaluation code converts $\\gamma_n$ to physical target units and multiplies both variances by the square of the inverse target-scaling factor. Square roots have target units; variances have squared target units.\n"
