-    "### Questions? Email John Schreck (schreck@ucar.edu)"
+    "## Figure 10-style Student-t uncertainty diagnostics\n",
+    "\n",
+    "This three-panel view follows the layout of **Figure 10(a) of [Schreck et al. (2024)](https://doi.org/10.1175/AIES-D-23-0093.1)** and uses the NIG mathematics underlying Equation (15). It evaluates the same trained evidential model on **every held-out observation**, with no new training or target. The original example predicts friction velocity only. The distribution used for the paper's published Figure 10 has not been conclusively traced; this figure explicitly uses Student-t CDFs.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Use the paper's random mean $\\mu_n$, predicted location $\\gamma_n$, and observation $y_n$. For this diagnostic, define the noise $\\epsilon_n=y_n-\\mu_n$. The NIG hierarchy in **Eqs. (12)–(14)** is\n",
+    "\n",
+    "$$\\sigma_n^2\\sim\\operatorname{InvGamma}(\\alpha_n,\\beta_n),\\qquad \\mu_n\\mid\\sigma_n^2\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\gamma_n,\\sigma_n^2/\\nu_n),\\qquad \\epsilon_n\\mid\\sigma_n^2\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,\\sigma_n^2).$$\n",
+    "\n",
+    "The mean deviation $\\mu_n-\\gamma_n$ and noise $\\epsilon_n$ are conditionally independent given $\\sigma_n^2$, but share that random variance. Their marginal variances are $\\operatorname{Var}(\\mu_n)$ and $\\mathbb{E}[\\sigma_n^2]$, as in **Eqs. (26) and (25)**. All three marginal distributions below are Student-t with $2\\alpha_n$ degrees of freedom:\n",
+    "\n",
+    "| Panel | Marginal quantity defining the reference distribution | Student-t scale squared |\n",
+    "| --- | --- | --- |\n",
+    "| Aleatoric reference | Noise $\\epsilon_n$; variance $\\mathbb{E}[\\sigma_n^2]$ | $s_{\\mathrm{ale},n}^2=\\beta_n/\\alpha_n=\\mathbb{E}[\\sigma_n^2](\\alpha_n-1)/\\alpha_n$ |\n",
+    "| Epistemic reference | Mean deviation $\\mu_n-\\gamma_n$; variance $\\operatorname{Var}(\\mu_n)$ | $s_{\\mathrm{epi},n}^2=\\beta_n/(\\alpha_n\\nu_n)=\\operatorname{Var}(\\mu_n)(\\alpha_n-1)/\\alpha_n$ |\n",
+    "| Total predictive PIT | Observed residual $y_n-\\gamma_n$; variance $\\mathbb{E}[\\sigma_n^2]+\\operatorname{Var}(\\mu_n)$ | $s_{\\mathrm{tot},n}^2=\\beta_n(1+\\nu_n)/(\\nu_n\\alpha_n)$ |\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Here $s_{j,n}$ is an auxiliary symbol for the SciPy Student-t scale in panel $j\\in\\{\\mathrm{ale},\\mathrm{epi},\\mathrm{tot}\\}$. The plotted CDF value is\n",
+    "\n",
+    "$$u_{j,n}=T_{2\\alpha_n}\\!\\left(\\frac{y_n-\\gamma_n}{s_{j,n}}\\right).$$\n",
+    "\n",
+    "The second Student-t argument written in the paper's **Eq. (15)** is $\\beta_n(1+\\nu_n)/(\\nu_n\\alpha_n)$. In SciPy's `scale` convention, this is $s_{\\mathrm{tot},n}^2$, so the code takes its square root. Neither this squared scale nor the scale itself should be confused with the predictive variance or standard deviation. Computations use the inverse-scaled predicted location and variances in physical units.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "**Interpretation:** the observed residual contains both components. The first two panels therefore compare it with component reference distributions; they are not PITs of separately observed noise or latent means. **Only the total panel has uniformity as a predictive-calibration target.** Component histograms can be nonuniform even for a calibrated model. The CDF values do not add; the variances do. A flat total histogram alone does not validate the component split or conditional calibration.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Nine equal-width bins and relative frequency (count divided by test-set size) follow the authors' released plotting convention. Each panel's bar heights sum to one; the uniform reference is $1/9$. The probability axis is appropriate for this distributional diagnostic. The earlier time-series and diurnal plots retain actual timestamps. The new total values are exactly the same as the predictive PIT above, displayed with this different binning and normalization. PNG, SVG, per-observation CDF values, and bin counts are saved below.\n"
    ]
